# [GENDISPATCH](../wg/gendispatch.html)

## Summary

The GENDISPATCH Working Group met to discuss a proposal on formalizing and clarifying consensus decision-making processes in IETF working groups. The presentation, based on [Making Decisions in IETF Working Groups](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-gendispatch-making-decisions-in-ietf-working-groups-00), was delivered by Mark Nottingham and Pete Resnick. The session was chaired by Martin Vigoureux (with Shuping Peng co-chairing remotely). 

The community discussed the appropriate dispatch venue, the intended document status (BCP), the balance between guiding and over-constraining working group chairs, and methods for ensuring broad community awareness.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### Presentation: Making Decisions in IETF Working Groups
- **Presenters:** Mark Nottingham and Pete Resnick
- **Slides:** [Making Decisions in IETF Working Groups](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-gendispatch-making-decisions-in-ietf-working-groups-00)
- Mark Nottingham and Pete Resnick presented a proposal to document practical recipes and guidelines for determining working group consensus, building on RFC 7282.
- The document aims to provide actionable checklists for chairs and participants regarding authority, determining support, handling technical objections, and trade-off decisions, targeted as a Best Current Practice (BCP).

### Roles of Chairs vs. Working Group Consensus
- **Robert Sparks** cautioned against over-constraining chairs or eliminating the latitude that allows "rough consensus" to remain flexible. He advocated providing guidance and support rather than rigid constraints.
- **Eric Rescorla (EKR)** argued that evaluating consensus is independent of judging technical merit. Chairs assess whether the working group has addressed objections, rather than inserting their own technical preferences.
- **Joel Halpern**, **Nick Doty**, and **Michael StJohns** discussed (in chat and on mic) the chair's responsibility in ensuring technical objections are substantively addressed by the working group without turning consensus calls into arbitrary technical judgments.
- **Eric Rescorla (EKR)** also highlighted the need for chair guidance on managing "astroturfing" and sudden drop-in participation by inactive contributors.

### Participant Definition and AI Contributions
- **John Klensin** and **Bron Gondwana** noted that consensus rules must account for intermittent contributors and clearly define what constitutes an active participant.
- **Pete Resnick** and **Bron Gondwana** noted that contributions from AI tools and defining human participation are increasingly relevant to this work.

### Dispatch Venue and Process Options
- **AD Sponsorship:** Area Director Roman Danyliw stated that this work will **not** be AD-sponsored, as cross-cutting procedural updates require full working group consensus and review.
- **PROCON Integration vs. Dedicated WG / BOF:**
  - **David Schinazi** suggested dispatching the work to the Process & Organization Consensus (PROCON) WG, noting its ongoing work on consolidating standard processes (updating RFC 2418 and RFC 2026).
  - **Jari Arkko** (PROCON Chair) and **Roman Danyliw** clarified that PROCON must complete its current consolidation effort (RFC 2418bis / RFC 2026bis) before rechartering to handle new process evolutions (targeted for late 2025 / early 2026).
  - **Cullen Jennings**, **Phillip Hallam-Baker**, and **Jean-Francois Tremblay** argued for holding a BOF or dedicated high-visibility session to prevent community surprise or backlash over core process changes.
  - **Barry Leiba**, **Eric Rescorla (EKR)**, and **Bron Gondwana** agreed on the necessity of widespread visibility, but suggested using plenary updates, mailing list announcements (e.g., `ietf-announce`), or dedicated sessions rather than requiring a formal BOF.

---

## Decisions and Action Items

- **Dispatch Recommendation:** The effort will be dispatched to an IETF Working Group. The primary target venue is **PROCON** after it completes its current consolidation milestones and undergoes a recharter.
- **AD Sponsorship:** Explicitly ruled out by Area Director Roman Danyliw.

---

## Next Steps

1. **Document & Charter Iteration:** Authors (Mark Nottingham and Pete Resnick) will continue refining the draft and drafting proposed charter text in consultation with interested community members.
2. **Interim Discussion Venue:** AD Roman Danyliw will coordinate with the PROCON chairs to establish a temporary discussion channel or mailing list for refining the charter text until PROCON is ready to recharter.
3. **Community Awareness:** The AD and chairs will ensure broad community notification (including plenary updates and announcement lists) regarding the scope and timeline of the rechartering effort.